# What keeps an archive out of reach of the thing that made it is that a name
# this may release is one path segment. An id with a slash could name a branch
# under escalated/ — an archive, or the record of a run that failed.
#
# It is now refused a step earlier than it was, and by a wider rule: a task file
# is named for the id it carries, and no name under tasks/*.md carries a slash,
# so a queue offering such an id is refused before any subcommand acts on it.
# Every path to a release reads the queue first and only releases an id the
# queue holds, so this is what an id with a slash meets from now on. The guard
# in release_claim is kept below it, unreachable from here and cheap to keep.
run release escalated/M1-01-swept-20260804-033732 82d2ac72741f2ad73921d3d11be2d040416cfdae 2026-08-04T03:37:32Z
exit 3
claims escalated/M1-01-swept-20260804-033732 main
file tasks/M1-01.md
	---
	id: escalated/M1-01-swept-20260804-033732
	depends_on: []
	exclusive: no
	---
stderr
	tasks.sh: tasks/M1-01.md: frontmatter says id escalated/M1-01-swept-20260804-033732, and a task file is named for the id it carries
